#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""tools/vtable-layout -- the header walk that types every interface's slots
from its own declaration.

    tools/vtable-layout               walk and type every row, and print one
                                      line per interface
    tools/vtable-layout --self-test   check this script's own reporting

    exit 0   every row is typed from its header and every policy key names a
             declaration
    exit 1   a policy key names a method its class does not declare
    exit 2   a header could not be read, or a slot could not be typed or
             accounted for

A LIBRARY FIRST, A CHECK SECOND
===============================

Two generators load this file as a module and read the rows and the walk
below. Neither keeps a header walk or a type map of its own, so the three
cannot disagree about a slot:

  * tools/engine-forwarders reads INTERFACES, chain_steps / derive_chain and
    signature_of. It writes one native forwarder per engine or shim method
    managed calls -- an ordinary virtual call, which native compiles -- and
    the C# function-pointer type of each, from the declaration walked here.
    A row's `struct` names the forwarder table, the last class of its
    `chain` is the forwarder's self type, its `header` is the include, and
    its `suppress` lists the methods whose forwarders skip the GC transition.
  * tools/managed-areas reads signature_of, normalise_signature,
    normalise_parameter and slot_hash. It writes both sides of every table
    managed builds from docs/managed-interfaces.tsv, typing each slot with
    the same map a forwarder is typed with and hashing that type for
    native's Validate.

So what a row here claims is each declared method's C signature, in
declaration order, with overrides folded into the slot they override. It is
NOT a claim about a struct managed indexes: managed reads no vtable by layout.
That is also why nothing here numbers a table the way MSVC does (one slot for
a virtual destructor, overloads grouped and reversed): a forwarder is a
virtual call each compiler numbers for itself.

The check (no flags) walks every row and types every slot, so a header the
walk cannot follow, a type the map does not name, an untypeable slot with no
account of itself on an exposed interface, or a `suppress` entry naming no
slot fails here, by name, rather than as a forwarder that compiles and is
wrong.

THE CONTRACT THE WALK RESTS ON
==============================

    THE ENGINE FACTORY INTERFACES ARE STABLE AND DO NOT CHANGE UNLESS THE
    INTERFACE VERSION STRING CHANGES.

That is the convention Valve's own headers state. `CreateInterface` either
answers for "VEngineClient014" or it does not, and if it does, the layout is
the one that string names -- so the walk reads each header once, as the
engine was compiled against it, rather than deriving every slot several ways
and comparing the answers.

The assumption is made true rather than trusted. Native is compiled with the
engine's own defines -- for `cdll_int.h`, `USE_SDL` in and `NO_VOICE` out --
and DEFINES below evaluates the same conditionals, so a declaration under a
`#if` that is false in that build occupies no slot here either, and one under
a condition DEFINES cannot decide is refused rather than guessed.

EVERY SLOT IS TYPED
===================

Every slot gets a real `delegate* unmanaged<...>`, derived from the header's
own parameter list AND its return type. The type describes the ABI; it does
not decide what may be called.

`Refused_` means exactly one thing: MANAGED CANNOT EXPRESS THE CALL --
varargs, or a class passed by value that BY_VALUE_RETURNS does not name. On
an exposed interface such a slot needs an account of itself: a C++ proxy
taking marshalable parameters (PROXIES), or a policy reason saying nothing
exposes it anyway, in which case a proxy would be dead code. A refusal whose
only trace is a deleted method is one somebody re-attempts.

POLICY IS NOT VERIFICATION. tools/vtable-policy.json says, per method, why a
slot this tree CAN type is still not exposed. It never changes a slot's type.
Here it accounts for a refused slot, and the check requires every key to name
a declaration of the class it is filed under, so a method renamed in a header
takes its reason with it instead of leaving a reason about nothing.

THE DESTRUCTOR PAIR
===================

An interface with a virtual destructor reserves its FIRST TWO slots on the
Itanium ABI, and the walk counts them uniformly for any such interface rather
than as a special case for one:

  Complete Object Destructor (D1) -- destroys the object's members and
  subobjects but does not free the memory.

  Deleting Destructor (D0) -- calls the complete object destructor (D1) and
  then invokes operator delete to free the dynamic memory.

The walk yields the pair as one `~destructor` declaration two slots wide.
Nothing forwards it, and nothing may call it from managed: slot 1 would free
the engine's singleton.
"""

